Hashcode is the numeric representation of object to store and retrieve objects efficiently
   commonly used by Hashmap, Hashset, Hashtable

 map.put(key, value);
   int hash = key.hashCode();              // 1. get hashCode
   int spread = hash ^ (hash >>> 16);      // 2. mix bits
   int index = (tableSize - 1) & spread;   // 3. calculate bucket index

int hash  - generally int has 32 bits hashcode uses this bits
>>> 16    - moves the upper 16 bits to lower 16 bits with 0 // line 15
hash ^    - XOR mixes with lower 16 bits for better distribution and reduce collision while allocating the buckets
tableSize - 1 & spread - picks the bucket efficiently

 hash        = 00000101 11100010 11100101 00100010
 hash>>>16   = 00000000 00000000 00000101 11100010
 -----------------------------------------------
 result      = 00000101 11100010 11100000 11000000 - XOR mix

0 ^ 0 = 0
1 ^ 1 = 0
0 ^ 1 = 1
1 ^ 0 = 1 - XOR

In general Hashcode is to determine the bucket where the object should live in based on the index

For collection-based - hashcode never works alone, it works along with the equals()
   - Hashcode - determine the bucket
   - equals() - checks if the KEY is already exits in the bucket